              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "**Mundra Port** is the **largest commercial and container port in India**. \n\n### 1. Location\nIt is located on the northern shores of the **Gulf of Kutch** near Mundra in the Kutch district of the state of **Gujarat, India**.\n\n### 2. Ownership and Management\nIt is India's first major private port. It is owned and operated by **Adani Ports and Special Economic Zone Limited (APSEZ)**, a subsidiary of the Adani Group.\n\n### 3. Key Characteristics & Significance\n* **Traffic Leader:** Mundra Port is the busiest commercial port in India. It handles over 155 million tonnes (and recently up to 200 million metric tonnes in a single year) of cargo annually, representing roughly 11% of India's entire maritime cargo.\n* **Container Hub:** It is a major hub for container shipping, handling roughly 33% (one-third) of India\u2019s total container traffic. \n* **Special Economic Zone (SEZ):** The port is integrated with a multi-product Special Economic Zone spanning over 100 square kilometers, which provides massive manufacturing, warehousing, and logistical advantages.\n* **Deep-Draft, All-Weather Port:** Because of its deep natural draft (allowing depths of up to 16\u201324 meters depending on the terminal), it can accommodate some of the world's largest ships. For example, in 2024, it successfully docked the *MSC Anna*, one of the largest container vessels to ever call at an Indian port.\n* **Connectivity:** The port is highly connected to the national rail network, highways, and major pipelines, making it the primary gateway for international trade heading to and from landlocked Northern and Northwestern India.",
